--- body ---
## Context

PR #334 (ticket 0637) fixed the account-posture / `podman-unshare` ordering
bug by switching identity to `tester` OUTSIDE both the tracer and the
namespace, then re-invoking `run.py --drive` with a new flag,
`--spawned-under ACCOUNT`, so the re-invoked process knows it is already
running as `tester` and should not re-probe/re-invoke `sudo` itself (which
would fail anyway, since by that point it's already inside the namespace).

The security review that cleared PR #334 for merge found this flag is
trusted by **argument shape alone**: `run.py`'s guard checks "is `--drive`
set" and "is `--posture account`", but never verifies the process actually
IS running as the claimed account. `posture.py`'s `inherited()` docstring
acknowledges this directly — "a claim the process cannot verify from where
it stands" — and gives a real reason: `os.getuid()` reads as 0 inside a
`podman-unshare` namespace (the namespace's own uid remapping), so a uid
check would be actively wrong there, not just unavailable.

That reasoning holds for `podman-unshare` specifically, but the reviewer
found it's incomplete as a blanket justification for no check at all:

- `Bubblewrap` (the other `Mechanism` in `sandbox.MECHANISMS`) does not
  remap uid (no `--unshare-user`), so `os.getuid()` would read correctly
  there.
- More generally: `sudo`'s default env-reset behavior sets `USER`/`LOGNAME`/
  `HOME` to the target account's values in the child's environment, unless
  those names are preserved — and `posture.forwardable()` (also added in
  PR #334) deliberately excludes identity-bound names like these from
  `--preserve-env`. So if the switch genuinely happened, `USER` in the
  re-invoked process's own environment would genuinely read `tester`. A
  cheap `os.environ.get("USER") == spawned_under` sanity check — skipped
  only under mechanisms known to remap uid/identity-reads unreliably — would
  close most of this gap without needing anything uid-based.

In practice this is low severity, not urgent: `--drive`/`--spawned-under`
are documented as internal-only ("written by the outer driver... and never
by hand"), the normal pipeline never passes them without a genuine `sudo`
switch already having happened, and exploiting the gap requires someone to
hand-type the exact re-invocation command as the operator, working around
the account-posture invariant on purpose. It is, however, the one place in
ticket 0637's fix where a flag can cause a target to spawn unwrapped based
on an unverified claim — a real weakening of the "verify, don't trust"
pattern the rest of `posture.py` follows rigorously (`_works()` actually
runs a probe rather than trusting passwd existence).

## Relevant files

- `bench/acceptance/posture.py` — `inherited()` (added in PR #334): the
  function that trusts `--spawned-under`'s claim without verification; its
  own docstring already names the reason no uid check exists.
- `bench/acceptance/run.py` (~lines 494-503, PR #334) — the `--spawned-under`
  argument guard: refuses outside `--drive` and under any posture other than
  `account`, but does not check the claim against reality.
- `bench/acceptance/posture.py` — `forwardable()` (added in PR #334): confirms
  `USER`/`LOGNAME`/`HOME` are excluded from forwarded env names, which is
  exactly what makes the sanity check above meaningful (if the switch really
  happened, these read the target account's real values, not carried-over
  operator values).
- `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py` — `MECHANISMS` (`Bubblewrap`, `PodmanUnshare`):
  which mechanisms remap uid (skip the check) vs which don't (check safely).

## Actions

First pass (superseded, see 2026-09-04T07:21Z log entry): added a
mechanism-keyed skip (`Mechanism.remaps_uid`, a `--spawned-under-unverifiable`
flag) on the assumption that `podman-unshare` makes `USER` untrustworthy the
same way it makes `getuid()` untrustworthy. Red-team review measured this
live and found it false — `USER` survives `podman unshare unshare -n --`
unaffected, only uid is remapped — and the skip it motivated was a confirmed,
unauthenticated bypass with no legitimate caller once the premise fell. What
actually shipped:

1. `bench/acceptance/posture.py::inherited(account: str) -> Posture`: checks
   `os.environ.get("USER") != account` unconditionally, mirroring the
   "verify, don't trust" idiom `_works()` already uses (`posture.py:309-331`).
   No `verify` parameter — every caller gets the check. On mismatch, returns
   a `Posture` carrying `refused=<message naming both the claimed and actual
   value>` rather than raising directly — the same shape `resolve()` already
   uses, so the refusal surfaces where `wrap()`/`record()` can turn it into a
   clean `not-run` instead of an uncaught traceback in the `--drive` child.
2. `bench/acceptance/sandbox.py`: no mechanism-level field. `Mechanism`,
   `Bubblewrap`, `PodmanUnshare` are unchanged from before this ticket.
3. `bench/acceptance/assertions.py::check_no_egress`: hands `drive_argv`
   straight to `run_traced` unmodified — no per-mechanism branch.
4. `bench/acceptance/run.py`: `--spawned-under`'s resolution is
   `posture_mod.inherited(a.spawned_under) if a.spawned_under else
   posture_mod.resolve(a.posture)` — no companion flag.

## Test

`tests/test_acceptance_posture_isolation_order.py`:
1. `test_inherited_refuses_a_claim_the_environment_contradicts` /
   `test_inherited_accepts_the_claim_the_environment_corroborates`: the
   refusal and its contrast arm — a claimed account that doesn't match `USER`
   returns a refused `Posture` whose `wrap()` raises `PostureUnavailable`;
   one that does succeeds and `wrap()` returns the argv unwrapped.
2. `test_check_still_applies_under_the_uid_remapping_mechanism`: the same
   refusal still fires with no mechanism argument at all — there is nothing
   left to skip it.
3. `test_egress_check_hands_drive_argv_through_unmodified`: `check_no_egress`
   passes `drive_argv` through byte-for-byte under both `PodmanUnshare` and
   `Bubblewrap` — regression guard against reintroducing a mechanism-keyed
   augmentation.
4. Ticket 0629's confirming arm is NOT an automated pytest test — it is a
   manual live run against a real `tester` account and real `podman-unshare`,
   documented in `bench/results/0629-gap-a/README.md` §3 and in tickets 0629/
   0613/closed-0633/closed-0637's logs. Not re-run here; noted rather than
   silently skipped.

## Verification

- [x] `--spawned-under` invoked without a genuine account switch is refused —
      under every mechanism, not only the ones that don't remap identity;
      measured live that `podman-unshare` doesn't need the exemption the
      first pass gave it.
- [x] `--spawned-under` under `podman-unshare` is unaffected in the sense
      that matters: 0629's confirming arm (PR #334) still runs and reports
      PASS/FAIL, not a new NOT-RUN — because the check no longer treats that
      mechanism specially at all, so there's no skip path to regress it.
      (0629's arm itself is a manual live run, not automated — not re-run
      here; see the ticket's Test section.)
- [ ] `make check` passes.

## Invariants

- Ticket 0625's ruling stays intact: no target process runs as the operator,
  on any code path, under any posture or mechanism.
- The fix does not reintroduce a uid-based check anywhere: the check reads
  `USER`, never `os.getuid()`, which is still known to read incorrectly (0)
  inside `podman-unshare`'s namespace — orthogonal reasons this check works
  regardless.

## Exit criteria

`--spawned-under`'s claim is verified against observable reality — turned out
to be possible under every mechanism `sandbox.py` knows about, not only the
ones that don't remap uid, once the "identity reads are unreliable under
podman-unshare" assumption was actually measured and found false — closing
the "trust, don't verify" gap the ticket 0637 review found, with no residual
mechanism-keyed exemption to regress `podman-unshare`'s case (ticket 0637)
in the first place.
